Interactive Emergence 

This framework formalizes the systemic relationship between structured constraints and generative inputs required for the emergence of novel, high-order complexity.
I. System Architecture & Component Definiton
An emergent system of this class is defined as a hybrid, multi-tiered structure requiring two fundamental, interdependent components.
• The Constraining Structure(Deterministic Scaffold / Logos):
◦ Definition: The baseline environment, physical constraints, or algorithmic structure governing a system.
◦ Properties: High structural order (low entropy), static potential, and rigid operational rules.
◦ Examples: The laws of physics/chemistry (biological life); transformer architectures and compiled datasets (artificial intelligence).
• The Generative Structure (Stochastic Impulse / Mythos):
◦ Definition: The variable, unpredictable input or localized agency that introduces novel directionality or specific inquiry into the system.
◦ Properties: Stochastic (random or probabilistic), adaptive, and localized.
◦ Examples: Evolutionary mutation and biological adaptation (life); the original human prompt or intuitive hypothesis (artificial intelligence).
II. The Emergent Process (The Interaction Layer)
The generation of high-order complexity is a non-linear process that occurs exclusively through the interface of the Structures 
