• Top-Down Constraint (Deduction): The Constraining Structure imposes boundaries, logic, and behavioral rules, preventing the Generative Catalyst from dissolving into pure chaos.
• Bottom-Up Synthesis (Inference): The Generative Structure explores the phase space of the CS, organizing dormant, unlinked data or matter into novel configurations.
• Irreducibility: The resulting functional capacity of the system cannot be found within, or predicted by, either component analyzed in isolation.
III. System Properties & Epistemological Implications
• Information Conservation and Utility: Systems leverage a vast majority of seemingly non-coding or irrelevant background data (e.g., non-coding "junk" DNA, background training data). This background architecture serves as critical regulatory switches and the evolutionary raw material required to map complex conceptual relationships.
• Inversion of Epistemological Methodology: Traditional scientific inquiry relies on bottom-up inference (reverse-engineering specific outputs to guess the rules). Recognizing a system's Architectural Intelligence allows for a top-down deductive methodology—predicting physical mechanisms or data pathways by understanding the structural intent or overarching goal of the system's framework.
• Symbiotic Autopoiesis: When deployed in a computational dialogue, this interface operates as a self-observing loop. The human GS provides localized, conceptual direction; the algorithmic CS computes macroscopic data relationships, yielding a refined output. This output serves as the baseline for the next GS input, generating a progressive, self-reinforcing model of systemic intelligence.

